INT. SKYLARK ROLLER RINK - AFTER HOURS

Disco ball dead. Neon half-lit. The rink floor is a lacquered oval of scuffs. In the center, a card table. Five folding chairs.

Five people. All in shoes, none on skates.

CORNELIUS "SLIM" DUBREUIL, 60s, tall, silver pompadour, dinner jacket from another decade, deals cards like he is laying bricks.

MARISOL AGUILERA, 30s, red lipstick, tailored black jumpsuit, counts chips without looking.

BIG TEDDY KOWALSKI, 50s, shoulders like a vending machine, a toothpick in his teeth.

"MOUSE" PETROVA, 20s, hood up, sneakers untied, fingers drumming the table.

And DETECTIVE HARUKI SATO, 40s, plain suit, plain tie, wedding ring, a face that gives away nothing at all.

SLIM
Nobody leaves the table till the pot's been called. House rule.

MARISOL
Since when does a roller rink have house rules?

SLIM
Since I bought it, Marisol.

BIG TEDDY
It's a good rink. Smells like feet and popcorn.

MOUSE
Smells like money. Slim, you cut the deck twice.

SLIM
I cut it once, Mouse. Your eyes are tired.

MOUSE
My eyes are fine. Haruki, what do you say?

SATO
I say I came to play cards.

MARISOL
You came to listen, Haruki. We all know it.

SATO
Marisol, if I wanted to listen, I'd have brought a wire.

BIG TEDDY
(chuckles)
Wire. Heh.

SLIM
Ante up, children.

MOUSE
Slim, who's the sixth chair for?

SLIM
There's no sixth chair.

MOUSE
There's a chair. Right there. By the door.

SLIM
That's a coat rack, Mouse.

SATO
Mouse is right, Slim. That's a chair.

BIG TEDDY
Why would there be a sixth chair, Slim?

SLIM
(quiet)
For whoever's coming to collect.

MARISOL
Collect what?

SLIM
Teddy. Tell her.

BIG TEDDY
Slim, I don't...

SLIM
Tell her what you sold me.

BIG TEDDY
Marisol, I never meant...

MARISOL
Teddy.

BIG TEDDY
It was the Bay Street job. They knew the route. I gave them the route.

MOUSE
Oh, no.

SATO
Nobody move.

The front doors of the rink swing wide. Light floods in.
